<BaseLayout
  title="The Texas Pest Control Licence, Explained"
  description="Licence classes, the seven categories, continuing education and how to verify any Texas pest control company in about two minutes. Cited to 4 TAC ch. 7."
  path="/compliance/pest-control-licence/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Crumbs items={crumbs} />

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Compliance reference</p>
      <h1>The Texas pest control licence, explained</h1>
      <div class="measure"><AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} /></div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Most people have never had a reason to learn how pest control licensing works in Texas, and the
          industry does very little to explain it. That is a shame, because it is the one area where a
          customer can verify something conclusively for free, and because the distinctions turn out to
          matter a great deal when you are comparing two quotes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Structural pest control here is regulated by the Texas Department of Agriculture, under Title 4
          of the Texas Administrative Code, chapter 7, subchapter H. The subchapter runs to seven divisions
          covering licensing, compliance and enforcement, unlawful acts, treatment standards and the school
          IPM programme.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our number is <strong>TPCL {o.tpcl}</strong>, printed on every page of this site. Everything below
          explains what that number is, what it does and does not cover, and how to check it — on us and on
          anybody else you are considering.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How to verify a licence — the two-minute version</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Search the Texas Department of Agriculture's structural pest control licence records for the
          number you have been given. You are checking three things, in this order.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>One — does the licence exist.</strong> A number that returns nothing is the end of the
          conversation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two — is it current.</strong> Licences renew annually and renewal depends on continuing
          education being completed. A lapsed licence is not a paperwork inconvenience; an application made
          under one is an unlicensed application on your property.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Three — and this is the one nobody checks — do the categories cover the work.</strong>
          If you are being quoted a termite job, the Termite and Wood Destroying Insect Control category
          needs to be on the licence. If it is not, the quote is not a quote; it is a problem waiting to
          become yours. The same applies to lawn and weed spraying, which sits in two further categories of
          its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Do this for everybody, not just for us. Reviews can be bought and photographs can be stock. A
          licence record is a licence record, and it is the only free and conclusive check available in
          this trade.
        </p>
        <p>
          One caution on reading the results. A company holds a business licence, and the individuals
          working for it hold their own applicator or technician licences. Those are separate records. If
          the number you were given returns an individual rather than the company you are hiring, or the
          other way round, that is not necessarily wrong — but it is worth one more question about which
          business the person will be working for on your job.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you would rather not search, the Structural Pest Control Service can be reached on
          <strong>(866) 918-4481</strong>, or at Texas Department of Agriculture, P.O. Box 12847, Austin,
          TX 78711-2847 — the same jurisdiction statement that the rules require to appear on termite
          disclosure documents.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The seven licence classes</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Set out at 4 TAC §7.121. The differences are about supervision and accountability, and they
          describe very different things standing on your property.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Business licensee.</strong> The company. Any person engaged in structural pest control
          for compensation must secure a business licence in the business's operating name, for each
          business location including branch offices. Two conditions attach and both are continuous: the
          location must have a designated responsible certified commercial applicator, and the business
          must maintain current general liability insurance at all times. The business licence reflects only
          those categories in which at least one active certified applicator is actually employed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Responsible certified commercial applicator.</strong> The named individual the business
          designates, responsible for the training and supervision of all pest control operations at that
          location. The constraint worth knowing: a person may hold this role for only one business licence
          location. They can work as an ordinary certified applicator elsewhere, but they cannot be the
          responsible applicator in two places at once.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Certified commercial applicator.</strong> Licensed in one or more categories, and able to
          perform pest control services, identifications and control measures without direct supervision —
          though still under the general supervision of the responsible applicator. Limited to the
          categories held.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Technician.</strong> Licensed in one or more categories, and performs pest control
          services <em>under the direct supervision</em> of a certified applicator. That phrase is the
          whole distinction.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Apprentice.</strong> A registered sales or service employee completing the training
          required for a technician licence, who has not yet passed a technician exam. Registered to one
          business, minimum age sixteen.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Certified noncommercial applicator</strong> and <strong>responsible certified
          noncommercial applicator.</strong> The in-house equivalents, for employees of governmental
          entities, apartment buildings, day cares, hospitals, nursing homes, hotels and motels,
          warehouses, food-processing establishments and schools who do pest control on their own
          employer's property rather than for hire.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why it matters to you.</strong> When somebody arrives at your house, one of these
          describes them, and the customer almost never asks which. A technician is competent and legal and
          also, by definition, working under somebody else's supervision. Whether that supervisor has ever
          seen your property is a fair question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The seven categories</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Set out at 4 TAC §7.124, with the Department's own definitions. These are the boundary of what
          any licence holder may lawfully do.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Pest Control</strong> — the inspection or control of pests in and around structures, or
          pest animals which may invade homes, restaurants, stores and other buildings. Cockroaches, ants,
          fleas, flies, rodents, skunks and raccoons all sit here.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Termite and Wood Destroying Insect Control</strong> — the inspection or control of
          termites, beetles or other wood destroying insects, and wood preservation, in structures,
          including termites in nearby trees. Note the official name: it is not simply "termite", and it
          covers more than termites.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Lawn and Ornamental</strong> — the inspection or control of pests or diseases of trees,
          shrubs or other plantings.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Weed Control</strong> — the inspection or control of weeds in right-of-ways, around
          homes, in a park, or in and around structures.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Structural Fumigation</strong> — the inspection or control of pests through fumigation of
          structures not primarily intended to contain food, feed or grains.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Commodity Fumigation</strong> — fumigation of processed commodities, raw agricultural
          commodities, or structures normally used to contain commodities.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Wood Preservation</strong> — pest control involving the addition of preservatives to wood
          products, including the re-treatment of power-line poles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What this business holds.</strong> {o.categories.join(', ')} — four of the seven. That
          covers essentially everything a residential or light commercial property in this region needs.
          It does <em>not</em> cover either fumigation category, which is why this site says plainly that
          we do not fumigate and do not offer bed bug heat treatment, and why a structure that genuinely
          needs tenting needs a different company.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And the arithmetic of holding four.</strong> Under §7.134 a certified applicator needs two
          general-training units plus one unit in every category held, every calendar year. Four categories
          is six units a year, indefinitely, as a condition of the licence staying valid.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What it takes to get one</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          Worth knowing because it tells you what the number represents.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>An apprentice</strong> registers with a business within ten days of starting, must be at
          least sixteen, and may not work without a licensed technician or certified applicator physically
          present until the required training is documented. That training is two classroom hours in each
          of ten general-standards subjects — twenty hours — plus forty hours of verifiable on-the-job
          training and eight classroom hours in each licence category. After the training is documented, a
          certified applicator must still be physically present for personal instruction three days a week.
          The apprentice period caps at twelve months, after which all of the training must be repeated.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The technician exam</strong> has a pass mark of seventy percent. Adding a further category
          to a technician licence takes another eight classroom hours and forty hours on the job. Renewal
          requires the supervising certified applicator to certify eight hours of verifiable training in the
          preceding calendar year, of which at most two may be on-the-job.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A certified commercial applicator</strong> must pass the appropriate category examination
          <em>and</em> a separate general standards examination — and then satisfy one of several experience
          routes. The ordinary one is verifiable employment in the pest control industry under the
          supervision of a licensed certified applicator for at least twelve of the past twenty-four months,
          while having held a technician's licence for at least six months in that period. The alternatives
          are documented equivalent out-of-state experience, a degree or certificate in a biological science
          related to pest control from an accredited two- or four-year institution, or having previously
          held the licence.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The business licence has its own conditions</strong>, and they are ongoing rather than
          one-time. Section 7.121(a) requires the licensee to maintain both the designated responsible
          certified applicator and current general liability insurance at all times. Section 7.129 covers
          what happens when a business loses its responsible applicator, which tells you the Department
          treats that as a live compliance event rather than an administrative footnote.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>None of it is a one-off.</strong> Renewal is annual and conditional on the CEUs. Which is
          the point of the next section.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Continuing education, and why it is a consumer issue</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          4 TAC §7.134 governs this, and it is more demanding than most customers assume.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Two general units plus one per category, per calendar year</strong>, certified at annual
          renewal. At least one of the general units must cover federal and state laws, pesticide safety,
          environmental protection, or integrated pest management.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>No repeats.</strong> An approved course may not be repeated for credit within the same
          calendar year, and instructors cannot claim credit for courses they themselves teach.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Self-study is deliberately limited.</strong> The rule permits CEUs by approved self-study
          course only every other year — self-study will not be accepted two years running, and doing so
          results in a deficiency on review. The intention is plainly that a licence holder gets in front
          of live instruction regularly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Records are kept for two calendar years</strong>, and a licensee found non-compliant has
          twenty days to produce them, with suspension in the deficient category available if they cannot.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The exemption for a first year.</strong> First-year licensees, and those newly certified
          in an added category, are exempt for their initial period — which is sensible, because they have
          just sat the examination that the CEUs exist to keep current. The Commissioner may also grant
          hardship extensions.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why any of this concerns you.</strong> Pesticide labels change. Products are withdrawn and
          replaced. Resistance patterns shift. Regulations are amended — several of the rules cited on this
          page were amended as recently as January 2023. A licence that renewed last month represents
          somebody who has been kept current on all of that, and a licence that lapsed represents somebody
          who has not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Questions worth asking any company, in order</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          These take about four minutes on the phone and they separate operators more reliably than
          anything else a customer can do.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"What is your licence number?"</strong> There is one right answer and it is a number.
          Hesitation, deflection, or an offer to bring it on the day is the finding.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Which categories are on it?"</strong> The question almost nobody asks and the one that
          most often matters. Match the categories against the work being quoted, not against the company's
          general description of itself.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Who will actually be doing the work, and what licence do they hold?"</strong> A certified
          applicator, a technician under direct supervision, or an apprentice are three different answers.
          None of them is wrong, and you are entitled to know which one is coming.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Are you the responsible certified applicator for this location?"</strong> Useful with
          small companies. Under §7.121(b) that role belongs to one named person per business location.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Can I have a certificate of insurance?"</strong> Maintaining current general liability
          cover is a continuing condition of the business licence. For commercial work you will usually need
          the certificate anyway; for residential work, asking is a fair test of whether it exists.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>"Will I get the Department's consumer information sheet?"</strong> For an indoor treatment
          at a private residence, 4 TAC §7.147 requires the Department's own sheet to be made available —
          a company may not substitute its own version, and it is published in English and Spanish.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>And for termite work: "Will the written estimate carry the disclosure documents?"</strong>
          4 TAC §7.174 requires them, sets out thirteen specific contents, and restricts their preparation
          to licence holders in the termite category. It also requires a written estimate before any
          treatment begins — a verbal figure may only be given as a general range.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Who regulates this, and what happens if something goes wrong</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>
          The Structural Pest Control Board that used to run this was abolished in 2007 and its functions
          moved to the Texas Department of Agriculture, where they sit today as the Structural Pest Control
          Service. If you find older material referring to the Board, that is why.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>TDA accepts and investigates consumer complaints against licensed businesses</strong>, by
          phone on (866) 918-4481 or in writing to spcs@TexasAgriculture.gov, and it publishes a complaint
          form. The mailing address is P.O. Box 12847, Austin, TX 78711-2847.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>What makes a complaint effective is paperwork</strong> — the service record, the products
          used, the dates, what you were told against what was written, and any sign or consumer information
          sheet you were given. Which is a good reason to keep all of it, whether or not you ever expect a
          dispute.
        </p>
        <p>
          We publish this because a guarantee that only works when the company feels like honouring it is
          not really a guarantee. The recourse exists whether or not a company mentions it, and a company
          that tells you about it up front has decided not to rely on you not knowing.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--ink-3);font-size:.95rem">
          This is a plain-language summary of published Texas rules, compiled for property owners. It is not
          legal advice. Rules are amended — the citations are printed so you can read the current text
          rather than trusting a summary on a pest control website, including this one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Verified facts={['LIC-01', 'LIC-02', 'LIC-03', 'INS-01', 'REG-01']} />

  <Faq items={faqs} heading="Licensing questions" />
  <Cta
    line="Ask us for the licence number and the categories before you book anything — then ask everybody else the same question. It is two minutes and it is the only check in this trade that settles the matter."
  />
</BaseLayout>
